## Key Takeaways & Summary for Systems Architects

The implementation of RFC-0112 introduces a paradigm shift in how distributed, multi-tenant robotic training pipelines establish trust. By combining append-only Merkle Mountain Ranges (MMRs) with non-interactive zero-knowledge proofs (zk-SNARKs), the protocol guarantees data provenance, historical immutability, and physical plausibility without compromising the proprietary nature of raw robotic trajectories or exposing sensitive edge environments.

### 1. Cryptographic Invariants & Security Bounds

The protocol enforces a dual-layer validation pipeline that operates under strict cryptographic and physical invariants:

```
+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|                                    RFC-0112 Protocol                                  |
+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
                                            |
       +------------------------------------+------------------------------------+
       |                                                                         |
       v                                                                         v
+--------------------------------------------+             +--------------------------------------------+
|        Layer 1: Temporal Integrity         |             |        Layer 2: Physical Feasibility       |
|  - Merkle Mountain Range (MMR)             |             |  - zk-SNARK Constraint System (R1CS/PlonK) |
|  - Logarithmic proof size: O(log N)        |             |  - Deterministic Kinematic Validation      |
|  - Append-only, collision-resistant        |             |  - Zero-knowledge property for trajectory  |
+--------------------------------------------+             +--------------------------------------------+
```

1. **Temporal and Sequential Integrity (Layer 1):** The state history of the training dataset is bound to an append-only MMR. For a dataset containing $N$ trajectory frames, any modification, deletion, or history-rewriting attack by a compromised storage node is detectable with $O(\log N)$ proof size. The collision resistance of the underlying hash function $\mathcal{H}$ guarantees that:
   $$\text{Pr}[\mathcal{A} \text{ outputs } (x, y) \text{ s.t. } \mathcal{H}(x) = \mathcal{H}(y) \text{ and } x \neq y] \le \text{negl}(\lambda)$$
2. **Physical Feasibility (Layer 2):** To prevent adversarial data poisoning (e.g., injecting physically impossible state transitions to corrupt the policy's gradient descent), the zk-SNARK circuit enforces kinematic bounds. The relation $\mathcal{R}_{\text{phys}}$ is defined as:
   $$\mathcal{R}_{\text{phys}} = \left\{ (x, w) \ \middle|\  \begin{array}{l} x = (\alpha_{\text{max}}, \Delta t, q_0, q_T), \\ w = (\{q_t, \dot{q}_t, \ddot{q}_t\}_{t=0}^T), \\ \text{and } \forall t \in [1, T]: \\ q_t - q_{t-1} - \dot{q}_{t-1}\Delta t - \frac{1}{2}\ddot{q}_t \Delta t^2 = 0 \\ \ddot{q}_t \Delta t - (\dot{q}_t - \dot{q}_{t-1}) = 0 \\ -\alpha_{\text{max}} \le \ddot{q}_t \le \alpha_{\text{max}} \end{array} \right\}$$
   Here, $x$ represents the public inputs (physical boundaries of the robot model, timestep, and boundary conditions), and $w$ represents the private witness (the actual high-frequency telemetry and joint trajectories). The zero-knowledge property ensures that $w$ is completely hidden from the verifier, preserving operational security and proprietary motion planning algorithms.

