### 3. Performance Profiles & Hardware Allocation

Architects must balance the trade-offs between proof generation time, proof size, and setup complexity when selecting the proving system:

| Proving System | Proof Size (Bytes) | Proving Time (100k Constraints) | Verification Time | Trusted Setup Required | Key Advantage |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Groth16** | $\sim 128$ | $\sim 1.2\text{ s}$ (Jetson Orin) | $< 2\text{ ms}$ | Yes (Per-circuit) | Minimal proof size & verification cost. |
| **PlonK** | $\sim 1000$ | $\sim 2.5\text{ s}$ (Jetson Orin) | $< 5\text{ ms}$ | Yes (Universal) | Custom gates simplify range checks. |
| **Halo2 (IPA)** | $\sim 4000$ | $\sim 4.1\text{ s}$ (Jetson Orin) | $\sim 15\text{ ms}$ | No | Eliminates trusted setup complexity. |

* **Memory Footprint:** For a standard 10-second trajectory sampled at 100 Hz (1,000 frames), the constraint system scales to approximately $450,000$ R1CS constraints. Proving requires a peak memory allocation of $\sim 1.8\text{ GB}$ of RAM, making it highly compatible with edge-level compute budgets.
* **Batching Strategy:** To minimize network transmission overhead, edge nodes should utilize **recursive proof composition** (e.g., via Halo2 or Plonky2). Instead of transmitting $N$ individual proofs for $N$ timesteps, proofs are folded sequentially into a single proof $\pi_{\text{final}}$ representing the validity of the entire training epoch.

