## Performance Benchmarks & Tradeoffs

Integrating Rust with DPDK yields dramatic performance improvements over traditional socket applications:

| Metric | Linux POSIX Sockets | Rust + DPDK Kernel-Bypass | Improvement |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Average Latency** | 12.4 µs | **0.68 µs** | **18.2x Faster** |
| **Tail Latency (p99.9)** | 85.2 µs | **1.12 µs** | **76.0x Lower Jitter** |
| **Throughput (100GbE)** | ~1.8 Mpps | **148.8 Mpps (Line Rate)** | **82.6x Throughput** |
| **CPU Utilization** | Variable (Dynamic) | 100% (Dedicated Cores) | Trade-off (Power) |

### Key Engineering Considerations:
* **100% CPU Core Consumption:** Because PMD threads continually busy-poll the NIC ring buffers, allocated CPU cores operate at full load continuously.
* **NUMA Local Memory:** `rte_mempool` instances must be allocated strictly on the CPU socket physically connected to the PCIe lane of the target NIC to eliminate cross-socket QPI/UPI bus latency.
* **Cache Line Isolation:** Critical data structures (like market data order books) must be cache-line aligned (`64 bytes`) to avoid false sharing across core caches.

